<h2 id="harvest">6. When to Pull Them (Beats Most Guides)</h2>

<p>This is where most people ruin it. You wait "30 days" from the packet. But the packet does not know your setup. The real signal is the top of the bulb pushing slightly above the soil line.</p>

<p>So here is what I do now: I gently brush a little soil aside around day 25 and peek. If I see a round radish the size of my thumb, it is time. I pull one as a test. If it is plump and crisp, I pull the rest. If it is still skinny, I give it 2-3 more days and check again.</p>

<p>Here is the honest truth. Radishes get woody, hot, and pithy if you leave them too long. Outdoors they can split from irregular watering. Indoors under LED, the enemy is time. A radish that is "almost ready" will be past its prime in 48 hours. Check daily near the end. Better to pull a slightly small crisp radish than a big hollow woody one.</p>

<p>And the leaves are not waste. Radish tops make a peppery salad green. I do not toss them. They are the bonus crop while the bulb develops.</p>

<p>The reward moment: the first time you slice a homegrown radish and it cracks that bright, crisp snap, with no bitterness, you will not go back to the bagged ones. I do not. It took me two failed batches and a month of pencil threads to get here. Let my failures save you the month.</p>

<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=cherry+belle+radish+seeds&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Radish seeds</a> run about $4-6. The <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Barrina+T5+LED+grow+light+4+pack&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">cool LED strip</a> is around $40 for four, and I already had it for my other crops. If you can keep the tray cool and feed it right, that is the whole secret. Check the seed pack, keep it under 72°F, and pull quick.</p>

